/// One step of a tenant operation (D-6546). Expressions are in the tenant rule language over the inputs (param), the subject record (record), the invoker (principal, has_role), now, balance, in_segment and setting. A later step reads the id of the record an earlier create-record step made as param.<step id> (D-6548). find-record reads the records of object the invoker may read (a record the invoker may not read is not a candidate, so its existence is not revealed); its predicate is evaluated with record as the candidate; it binds param.<id> to the one record that matches, or to null when none does, and refuses with error when more than one matches. branch runs its matched steps when its test holds, otherwise its unmatched steps; a branch holds only record and posting steps, not a find or another branch; post-ledger with reference names the record a posting is for (WG-5598), and its idempotency key is derived from the operation, the reference and the step id, so that reverse-ledger finds it again in a later invocation; reverse-ledger names a post-ledger step of the same operation by its id (posting) and reverses the entry that step made for the reference, posting the negated amount and naming the entry it corrects, under a key derived from the same reference; a reversal of a posting that was never made, or that was already reversed, is refused; test found is true when the find-record step named by the binding bound a record, and test condition is true when its expression holds. A binding made inside a branch is visible only in that branch's steps. write-setting (WG-5606) writes the setting (setting, a setting of the product) at the level (person, which takes the invoker's own value, or tenant, which needs setting-write-tenant) to the value the expression gives, under the rules the settings route applies: the definition's type, its allowed values, the setting's revision and one setting-changed event for the write.
